3a. Recalls that the creation of quality jobs is key to attracting a skilled workforce and incentivising employers to invest in workers’ skills development; recalls that recruitment difficulties and skills mismatches often stem from poor job quality, a lack of people-oriented human resources policies and untapped job design opportunities1a; calls on the Commission to address the issue through the quality jobs act and the Union of Skills by adapting upskilling and reskilling policies and introducing measures that promote better working conditions, occupational health and safety, and quality job creation, including in the digital and automated work environment; stresses that transparent algorithmic management and accountability are essential to quality employment; emphasises that job quality and decent work1b are essential for a healthy and resilient Europe and must be assessed through a comprehensive monitoring framework (such as those of Eurofound and the International Labour Organization) that goes beyond wages and employment security to include the physical and social work environment, working time, work intensity, skills and autonomy, career prospects, and access to training and reskilling;

---

1a European Parliament resolution of 15 March 2023 on the European Semester for economic policy coordination: Employment and social priorities for 2023 (OJ C, C/2023/402, 23.11.2023, ELI: [http://data.europa.eu/eli/C/2023/402/oj](http://data.europa.eu/eli/C/2023/402/oj)). 1b International Labour Organization ‘Decent work and the 2030 Agenda for sustainable development’ [https://www.ilo.org/sites/default/files/wcmsp5/groups/public/@europe/@ro-geneva/@ilo-lisbon/documents/event/wcms\_667247.pdf](https://www.ilo.org/sites/default/files/wcmsp5/groups/public/%40europe/%40ro-geneva/%40ilo-lisbon/documents/event/wcms_667247.pdf).
